WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.560
Thanks to the latest research
into violent expiratory events -

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.960
or, sneezes -

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.760
we can now observe
in very close detail

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.320
the “turbulent multiphase cloud”

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.960
which distributes a payload

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.800
of droplets and
mucosalivary ligaments,

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.360
or, well, stringy gobs of spit,

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.240
at high speeds
and over great distances.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.000
These gross but engrossing images

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.840
are made possible by highly
sensitive, slow-motion cameras

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.680
which can track
each single speck of snot.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.360
This is high-tech stuff, but
the fascination with sneezing

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.160
and the attempt to capture
and examine the sneeze

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.240
is actually as old as the
moving image itself.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:47.400
This short film, known as Fred Ott’s
Sneeze, was recorded in 1894

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.160
to test a new moving picture machine
called the Kinetoscope.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.440
This five-second long film,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.880
showing Fred Ott taking a pinch
of snuff and then sneezing,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.360
captured the imagination of a public

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.440
who were wowed by the idea that
something as fast as a sneeze

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.520
could now be captured, preserved
and repeated.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.320
Of course, sneezes were also
an object of fascination

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.200
long before the cinema.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.240
For the ancient Greeks, sneezes
could be an omen from the gods.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.880
According to one
ancient Roman doctor,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.240
sneezing during sex could
be used as a contraceptive.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.280
But the most common
interpretation of a sneeze

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.560
is probably that you might
be getting ill.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.280
But it was not until
the late 19th Century

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.480
that the idea that
infectious diseases

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.400
can be caused by microscopic
pathogens was introduced.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.120
It was this new understanding
of how disease is spread,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.840
along with the global shock
of the Spanish Flu pandemic in 1918,

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.240
that spawned the public health
messages that we know today,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.600
like, “Coughs and sneezes
spread diseases.”

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.920
It was the development of
stroboscopic photography in the 1930s

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.560
which really brought
this message home.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.440
This work, led by the MIT
researcher Harold Edgerton,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.280
used short sharp flashes of
light to seemingly freeze time

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.480
and analyse all sorts
of high-speed phenomena,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.320
including the humble sneeze.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.280
Images like this one helped us to
understand the raw power of sneezes,

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.440
and to think about how
we might protect ourselves

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:23.880
and those around us.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.880
An infectious sneeze can
be dangerous at any time.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.880
But the prospect of a
wave of viral infections

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.520
was particularly worrying to public
health officials in the UK

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.040
during the Second World War

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.720
who were keen to protect the health
of those working in the war effort.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.800
Wartime propaganda made good
use of these stroboscopic sneezes

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.200
to persuade people to cover
their mouths and wear masks.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.960
The germ mask is a simple
way of keeping the germs at bay.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.920
Get one and wear it now.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.200
In the age of SARS and COVID-19,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.520
cutting-edge technology
is still being used

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.760
to try to better capture
and visualise

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.560
the mechanics of the sneeze.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.760
Maybe an even fuller understanding
of exactly how a sneeze works

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.400
will help us to use tools like
masks and social distancing

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.440
more effectively in the fight
against epidemics. 

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.920
But it’s not only researchers
who are continuing

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.600
the long tradition
of filming sneezes.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.000
A quick search on
a video streaming site

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.600
will bring up homemade footage
from vloggers and filmmakers,

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.880
all capturing these explosive
moments for themselves. 

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.680
The image of the sneeze
is with us to stay.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.440
Yes, it can provide us
with useful information,

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.520
but it can also be a comic punchline,
a tool for persuasion,

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.360
or just a source of fascination. 

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.040
It’s something that
is common to us all,

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.760
yet it is also so strange,
spontaneous and fast

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.680
that we never really get to see it.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.320
Maybe capturing it on film,
repeating it, and slowing it down,

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.160
gives us a measure of control -

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.000
or at least the illusion of control -
that we can’t have in real time. 

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.160
And so in well over a century since
Fred Ott’s Sneeze,

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:09.640
in some respects

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.840
our viewing habits haven’t
really changed so much after all.

